"Coarser terrain","ui.coarse.title":"Coarser terrain in view","ui.coarse.km2":"about {{km2}} km\xB2: {{res}} m data ({{name}})","ui.coarse.help_button":"About coarser terrain","ui.coarse.help":"The detailed height data have no data in the marked areas, so their heights come from a coarser model. Narrow rock features are smoothed out at that resolution, so lines there may be missing or less exact, and a line may be found where the real rock has none.","ui.coarse.head":"{{percent}} % of the land here has only {{fallback}} data, coarser than {{source}}: lines there are less exact and some may be missing.","ui.coarse.show":"Show on map","ui.coarse.shares":"Heights of the land: {{shares}}","ui.shore.layer":"Uncertain shore","ui.shore.title":"Uncertain shore in view","ui.shore.surface_m":"{{m}} m: the foot of the cliff comes from the surface model","ui.shore.sea_m":"{{m}} m: the sea stands in for the foot of the cliff","ui.shore.help_button":"About the uncertain shore","ui.shore.chip":"Uncertain shore","ui.shore.km":"{{km}} km","ui.shore.m":"{{m}} m","ui.shore.unfold":"Uncertain shore in view, {{total}}. Show the details","ui.shore.fold":"Uncertain shore in view. Fold","ui.shore.help":"The height data stop above the water here: a laser scan from the air cannot see the bottom of a steep cliff. Orange, dashed: the bottom was taken from the second height model, the one that also shows trees and buildings; on bare rock that is the cliff itself. Red, solid: nothing could fill the gap, so the water's level stands in for the bottom, and the cliff looks as if it fell straight into the sea. In both cases the heights here are less sure than elsewhere. A line that starts, ends or passes here is worth checking on the spot. Such parts of a line count as filled (the Filled filter).","ui.view3d.imagery_failed":"{{failed}} of {{total}} imagery tiles could not be loaded.","ui.view3d.imagery_refused":"The imagery service does not answer: the terrain is shaded where its tiles are missing.",